            "input": {
              "filePath": "/private/tmp/tmplexzndny/pete/projects/ai-coding-guidelines/recommendations.json",
              "content": "{\n  \"stages\": {\n    \"prototype\": {\n      \"level\": 4,\n      \"rationale\": \"The whole point is to learn fast and throw the code away, so unread AI output is fine as long as it runs. Nothing here reaches real users, so defects and hidden complexity cost little.\"\n    },\n    \"proof-of-concept\": {\n      \"level\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"A live demo can fail in front of stakeholders or investors, so you need tests or manual checks on the paths you will show. Full acceptance (4) is risky because an unseen bug can sink the pitch, yet line-by-line review (2) is overkill for code that will be discarded.\"\n    },\n    \"mvp\": {\n      \"level\": 3,\n      \"rationale\": \"Real users depend on it, so behavior must be verified with tests even if you review selectively rather than line by line. This keeps speed high while catching the bugs that would burn early trust.\"\n    },\n    \"production\": {\n      \"level\": 2,\n      \"rationale\": \"Paying customers and a maintaining team mean every change needs a human to review it line by line for correctness, security, and maintainability. Selective review is too leaky when defects carry real cost and the code must outlive its authors.\"\n    },\n    \"critical\": {\n      \"level\": 1,\n      \"rationale\": \"Regulated or safety-critical systems need a human author of record and traceable reasoning for every line, so AI should only suggest and a person must write or rewrite the code. Full acceptance is unacceptable where failures can cause harm or legal exposure.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"summary\": \"Match the review rigor to the cost of a defect: lean into full vibe coding (4) only for throwaway prototypes, allow selective review with tests (3) for demos and MVPs where speed matters but behavior must work, require line-by-line human review (2) for production code a team maintains, and restrict AI to suggestions (1) for critical systems where every line needs human authorship and accountability. As a rule of thumb, drop one level for anything touching money, user data, security, or safety.\"\n}\n"
            },
